/-!
# Maximal subquasivarieties of a finitely generated group (Kourovka Notebook Problem 14.25)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 14.25 (A. I. Budkin): let `qG` be the
quasivariety generated by a group `G`. Is there a finitely generated group `G` such that the set of
proper maximal subquasivarieties of `qG` is infinite?

For each of a sequence of distinct primes `p > |A₅|`, let ℤ act by rotation on `A₅^p`.
In the product of these semidirect products, take the group generated by the simultaneous shift
and a copy of `A₅` supported at the origin of every cycle. Commutators with the shift by `p`
steps isolate the lamps on the cycle of length `p`, so each semidirect product embeds in the group.

Every homomorphism between distinct factors kills the finite base of its domain. For a fixed
factor, choose one quasi-identity for each nonidentity element of its base, asserting that every
homomorphism from the factor kills that element. Adjoining any one of these to the diagonal
group's theory excludes precisely this factor among the factors. A maximal class among the
finitely many choices is also maximal among all proper subquasivarieties.

Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 14.25 (A. I.
Budkin). UnsolvedMath record UM-00701010 (`KOU-14.25`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Quasivarieties are the classes of groups defined by sets of quasi-identities `∀ x (w₁ = 1 ∧ ⋯ ∧ wₖ =
1 → w = 1)`, compared on groups in `Type` (a quasivariety is determined by its countable members).
`qG` is the class defined by all quasi-identities of `G` (Mal'cev). "Infinitely many" is "for every
`k`, `k` pairwise distinct ones".
-/

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.QuasivarietyMaximalSubquasivarietiesKourovka14Problem25Wanted

/-- **Quasi-identities of groups.** A quasi-identity in $n$ unknowns $x_0, \dots, x_{n-1}$ is a
formula $\forall x\,(w_1 = 1 \wedge \dots \wedge w_k = 1 \to w = 1)$, given by the number $n$, a
finite list of premises $w_1, \dots, w_k$ and a conclusion $w$, all of them elements of the free
group on $x_0, \dots, x_{n-1}$. -/
structure QuasiIdentity where
  /-- The number of unknowns. -/
  n : ℕ
  /-- The premises `w₁, …, wₖ`. -/
  premises : List (FreeGroup (Fin n))
  /-- The conclusion `w`. -/
  conclusion : FreeGroup (Fin n)

/-- **Quasi-identities holding in a group.** A quasi-identity
$\forall x\,(w_1 = 1 \wedge \dots \wedge w_k = 1 \to w = 1)$ in the unknowns $x_0, \dots, x_{n-1}$
holds in a group $G$ when every assignment of elements of $G$ to the unknowns under which each
premise $w_1, \dots, w_k$ evaluates to $1$ in $G$ also makes the conclusion $w$ evaluate to $1$. -/
def QuasiIdentity.HoldsIn (q : QuasiIdentity) (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∀ x : Fin q.n → G, (∀ w ∈ q.premises, FreeGroup.lift x w = 1) →
    FreeGroup.lift x q.conclusion = 1

/-- **The quasivariety defined by a set of quasi-identities.** For a set $Q$ of quasi-identities,
a group $H$ belongs to the quasivariety defined by $Q$ when every quasi-identity in $Q$ holds in
$H$. Every quasivariety of groups has this form.

Only groups in the lowest universe are considered; a quasivariety is determined by its countable
members. -/
def Models (Q : Set QuasiIdentity) (H : Type) [Group H] : Prop :=
  ∀ q ∈ Q, q.HoldsIn H

/-- **The quasi-identities of a group.** The quasi-theory of a group $G$ is the set of all
quasi-identities that hold in $G$. The quasivariety defined by this set is the quasivariety $qG$
generated by $G$. -/
def quasiTheory (G : Type) [Group G] : Set QuasiIdentity :=
  {q | q.HoldsIn G}

/-- **Inclusion of quasivarieties.** For sets $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ of quasi-identities, the
quasivariety defined by $Q_1$ is contained in the quasivariety defined by $Q_2$: every group in
which all quasi-identities of $Q_1$ hold also satisfies all quasi-identities of $Q_2$.

Only groups in the lowest universe are considered; a quasivariety is determined by its countable
members. -/
def QVLe (Q₁ Q₂ : Set QuasiIdentity) : Prop :=
  ∀ (H : Type) [Group H], Models Q₁ H → Models Q₂ H

/-- **Maximal proper subquasivarieties.** Let $G$ be a group and let $qG$ be the quasivariety
generated by $G$, that is, the quasivariety defined by the set of all quasi-identities that hold in
$G$. A set $Q$ of quasi-identities defines a maximal proper subquasivariety of $qG$ when the
quasivariety defined by $Q$ is contained in $qG$ but does not contain $qG$, and every quasivariety
$V$ defined by a set of quasi-identities with $V$ containing the quasivariety defined by $Q$ and
contained in $qG$ either is contained in the quasivariety defined by $Q$ or contains $qG$. -/
def IsMaximalProperSubquasivariety (G : Type) [Group G] (Q : Set QuasiIdentity) : Prop :=
  QVLe Q (quasiTheory G) ∧ ¬ QVLe (quasiTheory G) Q ∧
    ∀ Q' : Set QuasiIdentity, QVLe Q Q' → QVLe Q' (quasiTheory G) →
      QVLe Q' Q ∨ QVLe (quasiTheory G) Q'

/-- **Budkin's problem on maximal subquasivarieties (Kourovka 14.25).** The statement that there
is a finitely generated group $G$ such that the quasivariety $qG$ generated by $G$ has infinitely
many maximal proper subquasivarieties: for every natural number $k$ there are sets of
quasi-identities $Q_0, \dots, Q_{k-1}$, each defining a maximal proper subquasivariety of $qG$,
such that for $i \ne j$ the quasivarieties defined by $Q_i$ and $Q_j$ are different. -/
def finitelyGeneratedGroupInfinitelyManyMaximalSubquasivarieties : Prop :=
  ∃ (G : Type) (_ : Group G), Group.FG G ∧
    ∀ k : ℕ, ∃ Q : Fin k → Set QuasiIdentity, (∀ i, IsMaximalProperSubquasivariety G (Q i)) ∧
      ∀ i j, i ≠ j → ¬ (QVLe (Q i) (Q j) ∧ QVLe (Q j) (Q i))

open Function
open scoped commutatorElement

private theorem lift_comp {I G H : Type} [Group G] [Group H] (f : G →* H)
    (x : I → G) (w : FreeGroup I) : f (FreeGroup.lift x w) = FreeGroup.lift (f ∘ x) w :=
  FreeGroup.lift_unique (f.comp (FreeGroup.lift x)) (by simp)

private theorem eval_map {q : QuasiIdentity} {G H : Type} [Group G] [Group H]
    (hq : q.HoldsIn H) (x : Fin q.n → G) (hx : ∀ w ∈ q.premises, FreeGroup.lift x w = 1)
    (f : G →* H) : f (FreeGroup.lift x q.conclusion) = 1 := by
  rw [lift_comp]
  apply hq
  intro w hw
  rw [← lift_comp, hx w hw, map_one]

private theorem holds_subgroup {q : QuasiIdentity} {G H : Type} [Group G] [Group H]
    (hq : q.HoldsIn H) (f : G →* H) (hf : Injective f) : q.HoldsIn G :=
  fun x hx => hf ((eval_map hq x hx f).trans (map_one f).symm)

-- A finite list of relations can be written with variables indexed by any finite type.
private theorem encode {I : Type} [Finite I] (r : Set (FreeGroup I)) (hr : r.Finite)
    (w : FreeGroup I) : ∃ q : QuasiIdentity, ∀ (H : Type) [Group H],
      q.HoldsIn H ↔ ∀ f : FreeGroup I →* H, (∀ v ∈ r, f v = 1) → f w = 1 := by
  classical
  let _ := Fintype.ofFinite I
  let e := Fintype.equivFin I
  let R := (hr.toFinset.image (FreeGroup.map e)).toList
  refine ⟨⟨_, R, FreeGroup.map e w⟩, ?_⟩
  intro H _
  have heval (x : Fin (Fintype.card I) → H) (v : FreeGroup I) :
      FreeGroup.lift x (FreeGroup.map e v) = FreeGroup.lift (x ∘ e) v :=
    FreeGroup.lift_unique ((FreeGroup.lift x).comp (FreeGroup.map e)) (by simp)
  constructor
  · intro h f hf
    let x := (f ∘ FreeGroup.of) ∘ e.symm
    have hx (v : FreeGroup I) : FreeGroup.lift (x ∘ e) v = f v :=
      (FreeGroup.lift_unique f (by simp [x])).symm
    rw [← hx, ← heval]
    apply h x
    simp only [R, Finset.mem_toList, Finset.mem_image, Set.Finite.mem_toFinset, forall_exists_index,
      and_imp, forall_apply_eq_imp_iff₂]
    intro v hv
    rw [heval, hx, hf v hv]
  · intro h x hx
    rw [heval]
    apply h
    intro v hv
    rw [← heval]
    apply hx
    simp only [R, Finset.mem_toList, Finset.mem_image, Set.Finite.mem_toFinset]
    exact ⟨v, hv, rfl⟩

private abbrev Z := Multiplicative ℤ
private abbrev oneStep : Z := Multiplicative.ofAdd 1

private theorem step_pow (z : Z) : oneStep ^ z.toAdd = z := by
  apply Multiplicative.toAdd.injective
  change z.toAdd • (1 : ℤ) = z.toAdd
  simp

-- Name every element of the finite base and one shift. The premises give its multiplication
-- table and the action of the shift, so assignments satisfying them extend to homomorphisms.
private theorem shift_diagram {B : Type} [Group B] [Finite B] (act : Z →* MulAut B) (b : B) :
    ∃ q : QuasiIdentity, ∀ (H : Type) [Group H], q.HoldsIn H ↔
      ∀ f : (B ⋊[act] Z) →* H, f (SemidirectProduct.inl b) = 1 := by
  classical
  let v (b : B) : FreeGroup (Option B) := FreeGroup.of (some b)
  let t : FreeGroup (Option B) := FreeGroup.of none
  let mulRel (p : B × B) := v p.1 * v p.2 / v (p.1 * p.2)
  let actRel (a : B) := t * v a * t⁻¹ / v (act oneStep a)
  obtain ⟨q, hq⟩ := encode (Set.range mulRel ∪ Set.range actRel)
    ((Set.finite_range _).union (Set.finite_range _)) (v b)
  refine ⟨q, fun H _ => (hq H).trans ?_⟩
  let gen : Option B → B ⋊[act] Z := fun
    | none => SemidirectProduct.inr oneStep
    | some a => SemidirectProduct.inl a
  constructor
  · intro h f
    let F := f.comp (FreeGroup.lift gen)
    suffices F (v b) = 1 by simpa [F, v, gen] using this
    apply h
    rintro _ (⟨⟨c, d⟩, rfl⟩ | ⟨c, rfl⟩)
    · simp [F, mulRel, v, gen]
    · simp [F, actRel, t, v, gen, SemidirectProduct.inl_aut]
  · intro h F hF
    let g : B →* H := MonoidHom.mk' (fun a => F (v a)) (by
      intro a c
      have hm := hF _ (Or.inl ⟨(a, c), rfl⟩)
      simp only [mulRel, map_div, map_mul, div_eq_one] at hm
      exact hm.symm)
    let s : Z →* H := zpowersHom H (F t)
    have step (a : B) : g (act oneStep a) = MulAut.conj (s oneStep) (g a) := by
      change F (v (act oneStep a)) = F t ^ (1 : ℤ) * F (v a) * (F t ^ (1 : ℤ))⁻¹
      have hm := hF _ (Or.inr ⟨a, rfl⟩)
      simp only [actRel, map_div, map_mul, map_inv, div_eq_one] at hm
      simpa only [zpow_one] using hm.symm
    have conj_all (z : Z) : ∀ a, g (act z a) = MulAut.conj (s z) (g a) := by
      have hz : z ∈ Subgroup.closure ({oneStep} : Set Z) :=
        Subgroup.mem_closure_singleton.mpr ⟨z.toAdd, step_pow z⟩
      induction hz using Subgroup.closure_induction with
      | mem x hx => simpa only [Set.mem_singleton_iff.mp hx] using step
      | one => simp
      | mul x y _ _ hx hy => simp only [map_mul, MulAut.mul_apply, hx, hy, forall_const]
      | inv x _ hx =>
        intro a
        apply (MulAut.conj (s x)).injective
        rw [← hx]
        simp [mul_assoc]
    change g b = 1
    simpa only [SemidirectProduct.lift_inl] using
      h (SemidirectProduct.lift g s (fun z => MonoidHom.ext (conj_all z)))

private abbrev A := alternatingGroup (Fin 5)

private theorem perfect_induction (K : Subgroup A) (h : ∀ a b : A, ⁅a, b⁆ ∈ K) (a : A) :
    a ∈ K := by
  have hK : commutator A ≤ K := Subgroup.commutator_le.mpr (fun x _ y _ => h x y)
  rw [commutator_alternatingGroup_eq_top (by simp)] at hK
  exact hK (Subgroup.mem_top _)

private noncomputable def period (i : ℕ) : ℕ := Nat.nth Nat.Prime (Nat.card A + 1 + i)

private theorem prime_period (i : ℕ) : (period i).Prime :=
  Nat.nth_mem_of_infinite Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime _

private theorem card_lt_period (i : ℕ) : Nat.card A < period i := by
  exact lt_of_lt_of_le (by omega : Nat.card A < Nat.card A + 1 + i)
    ((Nat.nth_strictMono Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime).id_le _)

private theorem period_inj : Injective period := fun i j h => by
  have := (Nat.nth_strictMono Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime).injective h
  exact Nat.add_left_cancel this

private noncomputable instance periodNeZero (i : ℕ) : NeZero (period i) :=
  ⟨(prime_period i).ne_zero⟩

private abbrev B (i : ℕ) := ZMod (period i) → A

private abbrev cycle (i : ℕ) := Multiplicative (ZMod (period i))

private noncomputable def rotation (i : ℕ) : cycle i →* MulAut (B i) where
  toFun z :=
    { toFun := fun a k => a (k - z.toAdd)
      invFun := fun a k => a (k + z.toAdd)
      left_inv := fun _ => by ext1 k; simp
      right_inv := fun _ => by ext1 k; simp
      map_mul' := fun _ _ => rfl }
  map_one' := by ext1 a; funext k; simp
  map_mul' z w := by ext1 a; funext k; simp [sub_sub]

private noncomputable def shift (i : ℕ) : Z →* MulAut (B i) :=
  (rotation i).comp (Int.castAddHom (ZMod (period i))).toMultiplicative

private abbrev W (i : ℕ) := B i ⋊[shift i] Z
private noncomputable abbrev lamp (i : ℕ) (k : ZMod (period i)) : A →* W i :=
  SemidirectProduct.inl.comp (MonoidHom.mulSingle (fun _ => A) k)

private theorem shift_lamp (i : ℕ) (z : Z) (k : ZMod (period i)) (a : A) :
    SemidirectProduct.inr z * lamp i k a * (SemidirectProduct.inr z)⁻¹ =
      lamp i (k + z.toAdd) a := by
  simp only [lamp, MonoidHom.comp_apply, MonoidHom.mulSingle_apply, ← map_inv,
    ← SemidirectProduct.inl_aut]
  apply congrArg SemidirectProduct.inl
  funext x
  change (Pi.mulSingle k a : B i) (x - z.toAdd) = (Pi.mulSingle (k + z.toAdd) a : B i) x
  by_cases hx : x = k + z.toAdd
  · subst x; simp
  · simp [Pi.mulSingle_eq_of_ne hx, Pi.mulSingle_eq_of_ne (mt sub_eq_iff_eq_add.mp hx)]

private abbrev Ambient := (i : ℕ) → W i
private noncomputable def walk : Z →* Ambient :=
  MonoidHom.pi fun _ => SemidirectProduct.inr
private noncomputable def lamps (a : A) : Ambient := fun i => lamp i 0 a
private noncomputable def diagonal : Subgroup Ambient :=
  Subgroup.closure (insert (walk oneStep) (Set.range lamps))

private theorem diagonal_fg : Group.FG diagonal :=
  (Group.fg_iff_subgroup_fg _).mpr ((Subgroup.fg_iff _).mpr
    ⟨_, rfl, (Set.finite_range _).insert _⟩)

private theorem walk_mem (z : Z) : walk z ∈ diagonal := by
  have h : walk oneStep ∈ diagonal := Subgroup.subset_closure (Set.mem_insert _ _)
  simpa only [← map_zpow, step_pow] using diagonal.zpow_mem h z.toAdd

private theorem lamps_mem (a : A) : lamps a ∈ diagonal :=
  Subgroup.subset_closure (Set.mem_insert_of_mem _ ⟨a, rfl⟩)

private theorem lamps_at_mem (z : Z) (a : A) :
    (fun i => lamp i (z.toAdd : ZMod (period i)) a) ∈ diagonal := by
  convert diagonal.mul_mem (diagonal.mul_mem (walk_mem z) (lamps_mem a))
    (diagonal.inv_mem (walk_mem z)) using 1
  funext i
  exact (by simpa [walk, lamps] using (shift_lamp i z 0 a).symm)

-- Conjugating a lamp by p steps brings it back to the origin precisely on the cycle of length p.
private theorem single_lamp_zero (i : ℕ) (a : A) :
    Pi.mulSingle i (lamp i 0 a) ∈ diagonal := by
  let e := (MonoidHom.mulSingle W i).comp (lamp i 0)
  change a ∈ diagonal.comap e
  apply perfect_induction
  intro x y
  have hx := lamps_mem x
  have hy := lamps_at_mem (Multiplicative.ofAdd (period i : ℤ)) y
  have h := diagonal.mul_mem (diagonal.mul_mem (diagonal.mul_mem hx hy) (diagonal.inv_mem hx))
    (diagonal.inv_mem hy)
  suffices e ⁅x, y⁆ = ⁅lamps x, fun j => lamp j (period i : ZMod (period j)) y⁆ by
    rw [Subgroup.mem_comap, this]
    simpa only [commutatorElement_def, toAdd_ofAdd, Int.cast_natCast] using h
  funext j
  by_cases hij : i = j
  · subst j
    simp [e, lamps, commutatorElement_def, Pi.mulSingle_mul, Pi.mulSingle_inv]
  · have hd := mt ((Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq (prime_period j) (prime_period i)).mp)
        (fun he => hij (period_inj he.symm))
    have hn : (period i : ZMod (period j)) ≠ 0 := mt (ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff ..).mp hd
    simp only [e, MonoidHom.comp_apply, MonoidHom.mulSingle_apply,
      Pi.mulSingle_eq_of_ne (Ne.symm hij), commutatorElement_def, Pi.mul_apply, Pi.inv_apply]
    change 1 = ⁅lamp j 0 x, lamp j (period i : ZMod (period j)) y⁆
    apply Eq.symm
    apply commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute.mpr
    exact (Pi.mulSingle_commute (f := fun _ => A) (Ne.symm hn) x y).map SemidirectProduct.inl

private theorem single_lamp_mem (i : ℕ) (k : ZMod (period i)) (a : A) :
    Pi.mulSingle i (lamp i k a) ∈ diagonal := by
  let z := Multiplicative.ofAdd (k.val : ℤ)
  convert diagonal.mul_mem (diagonal.mul_mem (walk_mem z) (single_lamp_zero i a))
    (diagonal.inv_mem (walk_mem z)) using 1
  funext j
  by_cases hij : j = i
  · subst j
    simpa [walk, z] using (shift_lamp i z 0 a).symm
  · simp [walk, Pi.mulSingle_eq_of_ne hij]

private theorem base_induction (i : ℕ) (S : Subgroup (B i))
    (h : ∀ k a, Pi.mulSingle k a ∈ S) (b : B i) : b ∈ S := by
  suffices ht : (⊤ : Submonoid (B i)) ≤ S.toSubmonoid from ht (Submonoid.mem_top _)
  rw [← Submonoid.pi_top, ← Submonoid.iSup_map_mulSingle]
  exact iSup_le fun k => Submonoid.map_le_iff_le_comap.mpr fun a _ => h k a

private theorem single_base_mem (i : ℕ) (b : B i) :
    Pi.mulSingle i (SemidirectProduct.inl b : W i) ∈ diagonal := by
  let f := (MonoidHom.mulSingle W i).comp SemidirectProduct.inl
  exact base_induction i (diagonal.comap f) (single_lamp_mem i) b

private noncomputable def incl (i : ℕ) : W i →* Ambient :=
  MonoidHom.pi fun j => if h : j = i then h ▸ MonoidHom.id (W i)
    else (SemidirectProduct.inr : Z →* W j).comp SemidirectProduct.rightHom

private theorem incl_at (i : ℕ) (x : W i) : incl i x i = x := by simp [incl]

private theorem incl_mem (i : ℕ) (x : W i) : incl i x ∈ diagonal := by
  convert diagonal.mul_mem (single_base_mem i x.left) (walk_mem x.right) using 1
  funext j
  by_cases h : j = i
  · subst j; simp [incl_at, walk]
  · simp [incl, walk, h]

private noncomputable def emb (i : ℕ) : W i →* diagonal :=
  (incl i).codRestrict _ (incl_mem i)

private theorem emb_inj (i : ℕ) : Injective (emb i) := by
  intro x y he
  simpa only [emb, MonoidHom.codRestrict_apply, incl_at] using
    congrArg (fun d : diagonal => d.val i) he

private noncomputable def reduce (i : ℕ) : W i →* (B i ⋊[rotation i] cycle i) :=
  SemidirectProduct.map (MonoidHom.id _) (Int.castAddHom (ZMod (period i))).toMultiplicative
    (fun _ => rfl)

private noncomputable def bound (i : ℕ) := Nat.card (B i ⋊[rotation i] cycle i)

private theorem bound_eq (i : ℕ) : bound i = Nat.card A ^ period i * period i := by
  rw [bound, Nat.card_congr SemidirectProduct.equivProd, Nat.card_prod, Nat.card_fun]
  simp [cycle, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, ZMod.card]

private theorem central_pow (j : ℕ) (g x : W j) : Commute (g ^ bound j) x := by
  have he : reduce j (g ^ bound j) = 1 := by rw [map_pow]; exact pow_card_eq_one'
  have h : reduce j (g ^ bound j * x) = reduce j (x * g ^ bound j) := by simp [he]
  apply SemidirectProduct.ext
  · exact congrArg (fun y : B j ⋊[rotation j] cycle j => y.left) h
  · simp [mul_comm]

private theorem bound_ne_zero_mod_period {i j : ℕ} (hij : i ≠ j) :
    (bound j : ZMod (period i)) ≠ 0 := by
  rw [ne_eq, ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff, bound_eq, (prime_period i).dvd_mul]
  refine not_or.mpr ⟨fun h => ?_, fun h => hij (period_inj ?_)⟩
  · have hd := (prime_period i).dvd_of_dvd_pow h
    exact (card_lt_period i).not_ge (Nat.le_of_dvd Nat.card_pos hd)
  · exact (Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq (prime_period i) (prime_period j)).mp h

-- In a different factor, a central power of the shift identifies two commuting lamps.
-- Their common image is abelian, and hence trivial by perfectness.
private theorem kills_base {i j : ℕ} (hij : i ≠ j) (f : W i →* W j) (b : B i) :
    f (SemidirectProduct.inl b) = 1 := by
  change b ∈ (f.comp SemidirectProduct.inl).ker
  apply base_induction
  intro k
  apply perfect_induction ((f.comp (lamp i k)).ker)
  intro x y
  let z := Multiplicative.ofAdd (bound j : ℤ)
  let t : W i := SemidirectProduct.inr oneStep
  have hz : z = oneStep ^ bound j := by
    apply Multiplicative.toAdd.injective
    change (bound j : ℤ) = bound j • (1 : ℤ)
    simp
  have hshift (a : A) : f (lamp i (k + (bound j : ZMod (period i))) a) = f (lamp i k a) := by
    have h := congrArg f (shift_lamp i z k a)
    have ht : f (SemidirectProduct.inr z) = (f t) ^ bound j := by rw [hz, map_pow, map_pow]
    rw [map_mul, map_mul, map_inv, ht, (central_pow j (f t) (f (lamp i k a))).eq,
      mul_assoc, mul_inv_cancel, mul_one] at h
    simpa only [z, toAdd_ofAdd, Int.cast_natCast] using h.symm
  rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, map_commutatorElement, commutatorElement_eq_one_iff_commute]
  change Commute (f (lamp i k x)) (f (lamp i k y))
  rw [← hshift y]
  have hn : k ≠ k + (bound j : ZMod (period i)) := by simpa using bound_ne_zero_mod_period hij
  exact ((Pi.mulSingle_commute (f := fun _ => A) hn x y).map SemidirectProduct.inl).map f

private def consequences (Q : Set QuasiIdentity) : Set QuasiIdentity :=
  {q | ∀ (H : Type) [Group H], Models Q H → q.HoldsIn H}

private theorem le_iff_consequences (Q R : Set QuasiIdentity) :
    QVLe Q R ↔ consequences R ⊆ consequences Q := by
  constructor
  · intro h q hq H _ hH
    exact hq H (h H hH)
  · intro h H _ hH q hq
    exact h (fun K _ hK => hK q hq) H hH

private theorem diagonal_models (Q : Set QuasiIdentity) (h : ∀ i, Models Q (W i)) :
    Models Q diagonal := by
  intro q hq x hx
  apply Subtype.ext
  funext i
  let f := (Pi.evalMonoidHom W i).comp diagonal.subtype
  exact eval_map (h i q hq) x hx f

private theorem factor_models (i : ℕ) : Models (quasiTheory diagonal) (W i) :=
  fun _ hq => holds_subgroup hq (emb i) (emb_inj i)

private theorem exists_maximal_omitting (i : ℕ) :
    ∃ Q, IsMaximalProperSubquasivariety diagonal Q ∧ ¬ Models Q (W i) ∧
      ∀ j, i ≠ j → Models Q (W j) := by
  classical
  choose d hd using shift_diagram (shift i)
  let T (b : B i) := insert (d b) (quasiTheory diagonal)
  have other (b : B i) (j : ℕ) (hij : i ≠ j) : Models (T b) (W j) := by
    intro q hq
    rcases hq with rfl | hq
    · exact (hd b _).mpr fun f => kills_base hij f b
    · exact factor_models j _ hq
  have omitted (b : B i) (hb : b ≠ 1) : ¬ Models (T b) (W i) := by
    intro hm
    have he := (hd b _).mp (hm (d b) (Set.mem_insert ..)) (MonoidHom.id _)
    exact hb (SemidirectProduct.inl_injective (he.trans (map_one _).symm))
  have hnt : Nontrivial A := alternatingGroup.nontrivial_of_three_le_card (by simp)
  obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := exists_ne (1 : B i)
  have hs : {b : B i | b ≠ 1}.Nonempty := ⟨v, hv⟩
  obtain ⟨b, hb, hmin⟩ := Set.Finite.exists_minimalFor (fun b => consequences (T b)) _
    (Set.toFinite _) hs
  refine ⟨T b, ⟨fun H _ hH q hq => hH q (Or.inr hq), ?_, ?_⟩, omitted b hb, other b⟩
  · intro h
    exact omitted b hb (h (W i) (factor_models i))
  intro R hTR hRG
  have hOther (j : ℕ) (hij : i ≠ j) : Models R (W j) := hTR _ (other b j hij)
  by_cases hi : Models R (W i)
  · right
    have hD := diagonal_models R (fun j => by
      by_cases hij : i = j
      · subst j; exact hi
      · exact hOther j hij)
    exact fun H _ hH q hq => hH q (hD q hq)
  · left
    -- A failed quasi-identity on the omitted factor gives a nonidentity conclusion c.
    -- The cyclic quotient still models R, so c belongs to the finite base.
    unfold Models QuasiIdentity.HoldsIn at hi
    push Not at hi
    obtain ⟨q, hq, x, hx, hne⟩ := hi
    let u := FreeGroup.lift x q.conclusion
    have hz : q.HoldsIn Z := holds_subgroup (hOther (i + 1) (by omega) q hq)
      SemidirectProduct.inr SemidirectProduct.inr_injective
    have hright : u.right = 1 := eval_map hz x hx SemidirectProduct.rightHom
    have hu : (SemidirectProduct.inl u.left : W i) = u := by
      apply SemidirectProduct.ext <;> simp [hright]
    have hc : u.left ≠ 1 := fun h => hne (hu.symm.trans (h ▸ map_one _))
    have hRT : QVLe R (T u.left) := by
      intro H _ hH r hr
      rcases hr with rfl | hr
      · exact (hd _ _).mpr fun f => (congrArg f hu).trans (eval_map (hH q hq) x hx f)
      · exact hRG H hH r hr
    have hle := ((le_iff_consequences _ _).mp hRT).trans ((le_iff_consequences _ _).mp hTR)
    have hback := (le_iff_consequences _ _).mpr (hmin hc hle)
    exact fun H _ hH => hback H (hRT H hH)

/--
**Some finitely generated group has infinitely many maximal subquasivarieties.** There is a
finitely generated group $G$ such that the quasivariety $qG$ generated by $G$ has infinitely many
maximal proper subquasivarieties: for every natural number $k$ there are sets of quasi-identities
$Q_0, \dots, Q_{k-1}$, each defining a maximal proper subquasivariety of $qG$, such that for
$i \ne j$ the quasivarieties defined by $Q_i$ and $Q_j$ are different.

This answers Kourovka Problem 14.25 positively; the answer is due to Bludov.

Source: E. I.
Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st
issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 14.25 (A. I. Budkin);
resolution: V. V. Bludov, Algebra and Logic 41 (2002), no. 1, 1-8, Kourovka Archive, Problem 14.25
(answer: Yes, it is).
-/
public theorem finitelyGeneratedGroupInfinitelyManyMaximalSubquasivarieties_holds :
    finitelyGeneratedGroupInfinitelyManyMaximalSubquasivarieties := by
  choose Q hQ using exists_maximal_omitting
  refine ⟨diagonal, inferInstance, diagonal_fg, fun k => ⟨fun i => Q i.val, ?_, ?_⟩⟩
  · exact fun i => (hQ i.val).1
  · intro i j hij he
    exact (hQ j.val).2.1 (he.1 _ ((hQ i.val).2.2 j.val (fun h => hij (Fin.ext h))))

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.QuasivarietyMaximalSubquasivarietiesKourovka14Problem25Wanted
